# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  What git is told to leave alone, and why.
#
#  Every entry is something this project actually produces. `start.bat` creates the Python
#  environment, the window's dependencies and the interface bundle; `npm run pack` and
#  `npm run dist` produce the packaged app. None of it is source, and all of it is reproduced by
#  running one of those, so none of it belongs in a commit.
#
#  Nothing has to be committed for the application to run from a clone: `start.bat` does every
#  step it finds missing, which is the point of it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Python: the environment, and what the interpreter writes as it runs ------------------------
#  `venv/` is created by `start.bat` and installed from `backend/requirements.txt`, which *is*
#  tracked. Committing the environment would put another machine's binaries in the history and
#  still not be the environment anyone else needs.
venv/
.venv/
env/
ENV/
#  Bytecode and the interpreter's own output: regenerated on import, and they embed absolute paths.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.Python

# --- Python packaging, if the engine is ever built into one ------------------------------------
build/
develop-eggs/
downloads/
eggs/
.eggs/
sdist/
wheels/
share/python-wheels/
*.egg-info/
.installed.cfg
*.egg
pip-wheel-metadata/
MANIFEST

# --- Tests and checkers: what running them leaves behind ---------------------------------------
#  `backend/tests` and `specs/features` are source and are tracked; these are the caches.
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.dmypy.json
.ruff_cache/
.tox/
.nox/
.hypothesis/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
coverage.xml
nosetests.xml
*.cover
.python-version

# --- Node and the interface --------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Both dependency trees are installed by `start.bat` from the two tracked lock files, which is
#  what makes an install reproducible. The lock files stay; the trees do not.
node_modules/
frontend/node_modules/
#  `frontend/dist` is the built interface, and the engine serves it to the window. `start.bat`
#  rebuilds it by itself when a source file is newer than the bundle, so it is never stale in a
#  clone for longer than one launch.
frontend/dist/
.vite/
frontend/.vite/
*.tsbuildinfo
.eslintcache

# --- The tools start.bat installs when a machine has none --------------------------------------
#  A portable Node and ffmpeg, and the archives they came from: about a quarter of a gigabyte, put
#  there by `scripts/bootstrap.ps1` for a machine that had neither. Committing it would put
#  somebody else's platform-specific binaries in the history for no gain; running start.bat again
#  reproduces all of it, which is the whole point of that file.
.tools/

# --- Packaging output --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  `electron-builder` writes here -- `build.directories.output` in package.json -- for the `--dir`
#  form and the installer alike: the unpacked app, the installer, its blockmap and the channel
#  files generated beside them.
/dist/
/release/
/out/
*.blockmap
builder-effective-config.yaml
electron-builder.env

# --- The application's own working files -------------------------------------------------------
#  A project file is a person's list of marks and belongs to them rather than to the repository.
#  The journal beside it is written while a project is open.
*.trimmerproj
*.trimmerproj-journal
/scratch/
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

#  What a cut writes beside its source: `default_output()` names it "<stem> HH.MM.SS.FF-HH.MM.SS.FF"
#  with the source's own extension, so a master dropped into this folder and trimmed here would
#  otherwise leave its segment sitting untracked, one `git add -A` away from the repository.
* [0-9][0-9].[0-9][0-9].[0-9][0-9].[0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9].[0-9][0-9].[0-9][0-9].[0-9][0-9].*
/media/

#  Where the engine's temporary pieces go, and why they are not listed above: `trim()` writes the
#  head, the body, the tail and the sound into a directory under the system temp folder
#  (`tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="thetrimmer-")`), joins them there and removes it. A cut leaves
#  nothing behind in the working tree, so there is nothing here to ignore for it.

# --- Editors -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
*.sublime-workspace
*.sublime-project
.project
.classpath
.settings/

# --- Operating system --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  `Thumbs.db` and `desktop.ini` are written into any folder Windows has been asked to draw,
#  including this one, just by looking at it.
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/
*.stackdump
